\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Schur multipliers and idempotency}

For matrices $A,B$ of the same shape write $(A \odot B)_{ij} = A_{ij}B_{ij}$ for the Hadamard
(entrywise) product. Fixing $A$ gives the \emph{Schur multiplier} $S_A : B \mapsto A \odot B$.
It is idempotent, $S_A \circ S_A = S_A$, exactly when $A_{ij}^{2} = A_{ij}$ for all $i,j$, that
is, exactly when $A$ is \emph{boolean}: all entries in $\{0,1\}$. Thus ``idempotent Schur
multiplier'' and ``zero--one matrix'' name the same objects.

By a classical theorem of Grothendieck--Haagerup type the operator norm of $S_A$ equals the
\emph{factorization norm} $\gtwo{A}$, which admits the purely Euclidean description recalled in
Section~\ref{sec:norm}. Everything below is phrased in those terms and is self-contained; no
operator-algebraic machinery is used.

\subsection{The conjecture}

A \emph{contractive} idempotent Schur multiplier is one with $\gtwo{A} \le 1$. Livshits'
description of these is that their symbols are exactly the blow-ups of partial identity matrices:
after permuting rows and columns, $A$ is block diagonal with all-ones rectangular blocks and zeros
elsewhere. The conjecture organizing the subject is the following.

\begin{conjecture}\label{conj:A}
For every $\gamma$ there is $L = L(\gamma)$, independent of the dimensions, such that every
boolean matrix $A$ with $\gtwo{A} \le \gamma$ can be written as
$A = \sum_{\ell=1}^{L} \varepsilon_{\ell} B_{\ell}$ with $\varepsilon_{\ell} \in \{\pm 1\}$ and
each $B_{\ell}$ a blow-up of a partial identity matrix.
\end{conjecture}

The best known bounds attain $L = 2^{O(\gamma^{9}) + \log^{*} n}$ for $n \times n$ matrices, where
$\log^{*}$ is the iterated logarithm; the conjecture asks that the $\log^{*} n$ be removed. As an
application, families of bounded factorization norm lie in the communication class
$\mathrm{P}^{\mathrm{EQ}}$, because a signed sum of $L$ blow-ups is literally a signed combination
of $L$ equality tests (Section~\ref{sec:eq}).

\subsection{Contributions}

We give a complete self-contained treatment of the bottom of the hierarchy together with the
algebraic structure of the class of signed sums of blow-ups: a three-way characterization of the
contractive case (Theorem~\ref{thm:char}); the exact norm of the $2 \times 2$ staircase
(Theorem~\ref{thm:exact}); a sharp gap (Theorem~\ref{thm:gap}); submultiplicativity and closure
properties (Section~\ref{sec:algebra}); and the blow-up number with the conjecture settled with
$L=1$ below the gap (Theorem~\ref{thm:belowgap}).

\section{The factorization norm}\label{sec:norm}

Throughout $A$ is a real $m \times n$ matrix; all inner products are the standard ones on
$\mathbb{R}^{d}$.

\begin{definition}[Factorization of size $c$]
A \emph{$\gamma_2$-factorization of $A$ of size $c$} consists of a dimension $d$ and vectors
$x_1,\dots,x_m,y_1,\dots,y_n \in \mathbb{R}^{d}$ with
\[
  \langle x_i, y_j\rangle = A_{ij} \ \ \forall i,j,
  \qquad \lVert x_i \rVert^{2} \le c \ \ \forall i,
  \qquad \lVert y_j \rVert^{2} \le c \ \ \forall j .
\]
We write $\gtwo{A} \le c$ when such a factorization exists.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Unbalanced form]
$\gtwo{A}' \le c$ means: there are $x_i,y_j$ with $\langle x_i,y_j\rangle = A_{ij}$ and
$(\max_i \lVert x_i\rVert)(\max_j \lVert y_j\rVert) \le c$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Equivalence]\label{prop:balance}
For $c \ge 0$, $\gtwo{A} \le c$ if and only if $\gtwo{A}' \le c$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
If all squared norms are at most $c$ then the product of the maxima is at most $c$. Conversely put
$X = \max_i \lVert x_i \rVert$, $Y = \max_j \lVert y_j\rVert$, $XY \le c$. If $X = 0$ or $Y = 0$
then $A = 0$. Otherwise rescale by $\lambda = \sqrt{Y/X}$: the inner products are unchanged, while
$\max_i \lVert \lambda x_i\rVert^{2} = XY \le c$ and
$\max_j \lVert \lambda^{-1} y_j\rVert^{2} = XY \le c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Elementary properties]\label{prop:elem}
Let $A,B$ be $m\times n$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item if $\gtwo{A}\le c \le c'$ then $\gtwo{A} \le c'$;
\item if $\gtwo{A}\le c$ then $|A_{ij}| \le c$ for all $i,j$;
\item $\gtwo{-A} = \gtwo{A}$;
\item $\gtwo{A+B} \le \gtwo{A} + \gtwo{B}$, and more generally
      $\gtwo{\sum_{\ell<L} C_{\ell}} \le \sum_{\ell<L}\gtwo{C_{\ell}}$;
\item $\gtwo{A^{\mathsf T}} = \gtwo{A}$;
\item for any maps $\sigma : [m'] \to [m]$, $\tau : [n'] \to [n]$ the submatrix
      $A' = (A_{\sigma(i)\tau(j)})$ satisfies $\gtwo{A'} \le \gtwo{A}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
(1), (5) and (6) are immediate from the definition (for (6), restrict the two families of vectors
along $\sigma$ and $\tau$). (2) is Cauchy--Schwarz:
$|A_{ij}| = |\langle x_i,y_j\rangle| \le \lVert x_i\rVert \lVert y_j\rVert \le c$. (3) negates the
$x$-family. For (4), concatenate: if $A_{ij} = \langle x_i,y_j\rangle$ in $\mathbb{R}^{d}$ with
bound $c$ and $B_{ij} = \langle u_i,v_j\rangle$ in $\mathbb{R}^{d'}$ with bound $c'$, then in
$\mathbb{R}^{d+d'}$ the vectors $(x_i,u_i)$ and $(y_j,v_j)$ have inner products $A_{ij}+B_{ij}$
and squared norms at most $c+c'$; induct for the general case.
\end{proof}

Property (6) is what makes forbidden-pattern arguments possible: a lower bound for a small pattern
propagates to every matrix containing it.

\section{Blow-ups, rigidity, and the contractive case}

\begin{definition}[Blow-up of a partial identity]
$A$ is a \emph{blow-up} if there are labellings $f : [m] \to \mathbb{N}$ and
$g : [n] \to \mathbb{N}$ with $A_{ij} = \bone[f(i) = g(j)]$.
\end{definition}

Grouping rows and columns by label exhibits $A$, after permutations, as a block-diagonal matrix
whose diagonal blocks are all-ones rectangles. Every blow-up is boolean.

\begin{proposition}[Idempotency]
$S_A \circ S_A = S_A$ if and only if $A$ is boolean.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$S_A(S_A(B))_{ij} = A_{ij}^{2}B_{ij}$; taking $B$ all-ones shows the condition is
$A_{ij}^{2} = A_{ij}$, i.e.\ $A_{ij}\in\{0,1\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Blow-ups are contractive]\label{prop:blowupcontr}
If $A$ is a blow-up then $\gtwo{A} \le 1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Choose $K$ larger than all labels and set $x_i = e_{f(i)}$, $y_j = e_{g(j)}$ in $\mathbb{R}^{K}$.
Then $\langle x_i,y_j\rangle = \bone[f(i)=g(j)] = A_{ij}$ and all squared norms equal $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Row rigidity]
$A$ is \emph{row rigid} if for all rows $i,i'$ and every column $j$,
\[
  A_{ij} = 1 \text{ and } A_{i'j} = 1 \implies A_{ij'} = A_{i'j'} \text{ for every column } j' .
\]
\end{definition}

For boolean matrices this is exactly the absence of the $2 \times 2$ pattern
$T_2 = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&1\\1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)$; see Lemma~\ref{lem:pattern}.

\begin{theorem}[Characterization of contractive idempotents]\label{thm:char}
For a boolean matrix $A$ the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\gtwo{A} \le 1$;
\item $A$ is a blow-up of a partial identity matrix;
\item $A$ is row rigid.
\end{enumerate}
Equivalently: $S_A$ is an idempotent Schur multiplier of norm at most $1$ if and only if $A$ is a
blow-up of a partial identity matrix.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(2)\Rightarrow(1)$ is Proposition~\ref{prop:blowupcontr}. $(2)\Rightarrow(3)$: if
$A_{ij} = A_{i'j} = 1$ then $f(i) = g(j) = f(i')$, so rows $i,i'$ carry the same label and hence
are the same indicator row.

$(1)\Rightarrow(3)$ is the equality case of Cauchy--Schwarz. Let $x_i,y_j$ realize
$\gtwo{A}\le 1$. If $A_{ij} = 1$ then
$1 = \langle x_i,y_j\rangle \le \lVert x_i\rVert\lVert y_j\rVert \le 1$, so
$\lVert x_i - y_j\rVert^{2} = \lVert x_i\rVert^{2} - 2\langle x_i,y_j\rangle + \lVert y_j\rVert^{2}
\le 1 - 2 + 1 = 0$ and $x_i = y_j$: a $1$ glues the two vectors. Hence
$A_{ij}=A_{i'j}=1$ gives $x_i = y_j = x_{i'}$ and therefore
$A_{ij'} = \langle x_i,y_{j'}\rangle = \langle x_{i'},y_{j'}\rangle = A_{i'j'}$ for all $j'$.

$(3)\Rightarrow(2)$ constructs labels. Let
$f(i) = \min(\{ j : A_{ij}=1\} \cup \{n+i\})$, the leftmost $1$ of row $i$ or a fresh tag if the
row is zero, and
$g(j) = \min(\{ j' : \exists i,\ A_{ij}=1 \wedge A_{ij'}=1\} \cup \{n+m+j\})$, the leftmost $1$ of
any row hitting column $j$, or a fresh tag if the column is zero. If $A_{ij}=1$ then rigidity makes
every row hitting $j$ equal to row $i$, so $g(j) = f(i)$. If $A_{ij}=0$ and column $j$ is zero then
$g(j)$ is a fresh tag exceeding all $f(i)$; if some $i_0$ hits $j$ then $g(j)=f(i_0)$, and
$f(i)=f(i_0)$ would place a common $1$ in rows $i,i_0$, whence rigidity would give
$A_{ij}=A_{i_0 j}=1$, a contradiction. Thus $A_{ij} = \bone[f(i)=g(j)]$.
\end{proof}

\section{The sharp gap at $2\sqrt{3}/3$}

Write $T_2 = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&1\\1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)$, the $2$-staircase.

\begin{theorem}[Sum-of-squares lower bound]\label{thm:sos}
Suppose $\gtwo{A}\le c$ and $A$ contains $T_2$ as a (not necessarily contiguous) $2\times 2$
submatrix: there are rows $i,i'$ and columns $j,j'$ with
$A_{ij}=A_{ij'}=A_{i'j}=1$ and $A_{i'j'}=0$. Then $c \ge 2\sqrt{3}/3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Put $a = x_i$, $b = x_{i'}$, $p = y_j$, $q = y_{j'}$, so that
$\langle a,p\rangle=\langle a,q\rangle=\langle b,p\rangle=1$, $\langle b,q\rangle=0$, and all four
squared norms are at most $c$. Consider
\[
  0 \;\le\; \bigl\lVert \sqrt{3}\,b - 2p + q \bigr\rVert^{2}
        + 2\bigl\lVert -\sqrt{3}\,a + p + q \bigr\rVert^{2}.
\]
Coordinatewise expansion (using $(\sqrt3)^2 = 3$) gives
\[
  (\sqrt3 b_t - 2p_t + q_t)^{2} + 2(-\sqrt3 a_t + p_t + q_t)^{2}
  = 6a_t^{2}+3b_t^{2}+6p_t^{2}+3q_t^{2}
   -4\sqrt3\,b_tp_t + 2\sqrt3\,b_tq_t - 4\sqrt3\,a_tp_t - 4\sqrt3\,a_tq_t .
\]
Summing over $t$ and inserting the four known inner products,
\[
  0 \le 6\lVert a\rVert^{2}+3\lVert b\rVert^{2}+6\lVert p\rVert^{2}+3\lVert q\rVert^{2}
       - 12\sqrt3 \le 18c - 12\sqrt3 ,
\]
whence $c \ge 12\sqrt3/18 = 2\sqrt3/3$.
\end{proof}

The coefficients are the optimal solution of the dual semidefinite program; the matching primal
certificate follows.

\begin{theorem}[Explicit optimal factorization]\label{thm:primal}
$\gtwo{T_2} \le 2\sqrt{3}/3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $c = 2\sqrt3/3$ and $r = \sqrt{c}$. In $\mathbb{R}^{2}$ set
\[
  x_1 = (r,0), \quad x_2 = \Bigl(\tfrac r2, \tfrac{r\sqrt3}{2}\Bigr), \quad
  y_1 = \Bigl(\tfrac{r\sqrt3}{2}, \tfrac r2\Bigr), \quad
  y_2 = \Bigl(\tfrac{r\sqrt3}{2}, -\tfrac r2\Bigr).
\]
All four have length $r$, i.e.\ squared norm exactly $c$, at arguments
$0^{\circ}, 60^{\circ}, 30^{\circ}, -30^{\circ}$: a fan at consecutive $30^{\circ}$ intervals in
the order $y_2, x_1, y_1, x_2$. Hence
$\langle x_1,y_1\rangle = \langle x_1,y_2\rangle = \langle x_2,y_1\rangle
= r^{2}\cos 30^{\circ} = c\sqrt3/2 = 1$, while $x_2 \perp y_2$ gives
$\langle x_2,y_2\rangle = 0$. This is exactly $T_2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact value]\label{thm:exact}
For every real $c$: $\gtwo{T_2}\le c$ if and only if $c \ge 2\sqrt3/3$. In particular
\[
  \left\lVert \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1\\ 1 & 0\end{pmatrix}\right\rVert_{\gamma_2}
   = \frac{2\sqrt3}{3} = \frac{2}{\sqrt3} = 1.15470\ldots
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Necessity is Theorem~\ref{thm:sos} applied to $T_2$; sufficiency is Theorem~\ref{thm:primal} with
monotonicity.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:pattern}
If $A$ is boolean and not row rigid then $A$ contains $T_2$ as a $2\times2$ submatrix.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Failure gives rows $i,i'$ and columns $j,j'$ with $A_{ij}=A_{i'j}=1$ and
$A_{ij'} \ne A_{i'j'}$. As $A$ is boolean, one of these two entries is $1$ and the other $0$;
order the two rows so that the $0$ sits in the second one.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Gap theorem]\label{thm:gap}
Let $A$ be boolean with $\gtwo{A} \le c$ and $c < 2\sqrt3/3$. Then $A$ is a blow-up of a partial
identity matrix; in particular $\gtwo{A} \le 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:char} it suffices to prove row rigidity. If it failed,
Lemma~\ref{lem:pattern} would produce a $T_2$ submatrix and Theorem~\ref{thm:sos} would give
$c \ge 2\sqrt3/3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Dichotomy and sharpness]
For every boolean $A$ and every $c$ with $\gtwo{A}\le c$: either $\gtwo{A}\le 1$ or
$c \ge 2\sqrt3/3$. Hence no boolean matrix of any size has $\gtwo{A} \in (1, 2\sqrt3/3)$. The right
endpoint is attained: $T_2$ is boolean, is not a blow-up, and $\gtwo{T_2} = 2\sqrt3/3$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[The next staircase]\label{prop:tri3}
Let $T_3 = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&1&1\\1&1&0\\1&0&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)$. Then $T_3$ is
boolean, is not a blow-up, and $2\sqrt3/3 \le \gtwo{T_3} \le \sqrt3$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Rows $2,3$ share a $1$ in column $1$ but differ in column $2$, so $T_3$ is not row rigid and, by
Theorem~\ref{thm:char}, not a blow-up; the same $2\times2$ pattern and Theorem~\ref{thm:sos} give
the lower bound. The upper bound is the general bound $\gtwo{A}\le\sqrt{\min(m,n)}$ of
Proposition~\ref{prop:universal}.
\end{proof}

\section{The algebra of blow-ups and of signed sums}\label{sec:algebra}

Composition of Schur multipliers is the Hadamard product of symbols: $S_A \circ S_B = S_{A\odot B}$.

\begin{theorem}[Submultiplicativity]\label{thm:submult}
If $\gtwo{A}\le c$ and $\gtwo{B}\le d$ with $c,d \ge 0$, then $\gtwo{A\odot B} \le cd$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $A_{ij}=\langle x_i,y_j\rangle$ and $B_{ij}=\langle u_i,v_j\rangle$ with squared norms bounded
by $c$ and $d$. In the tensor product, indexed by pairs $(s,s')$, put
$X_i = x_i \otimes u_i$ and $Y_j = y_j \otimes v_j$. Then
\[
  \langle X_i, Y_j\rangle
  = \sum_{s,s'} (x_i)_s (u_i)_{s'} (y_j)_s (v_j)_{s'}
  = \Bigl(\sum_s (x_i)_s (y_j)_s\Bigr)\Bigl(\sum_{s'}(u_i)_{s'}(v_j)_{s'}\Bigr)
  = A_{ij}B_{ij},
\]
and $\lVert X_i\rVert^{2} = \lVert x_i\rVert^{2}\lVert u_i\rVert^{2} \le cd$, similarly for $Y_j$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Blow-ups are closed under $\odot$]\label{thm:blowupclosed}
If $A$ and $B$ are blow-ups then so is $A \odot B$. Equivalently, contractive idempotent Schur
multipliers are closed under composition.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $A_{ij}=\bone[f_1(i)=g_1(j)]$ and $B_{ij}=\bone[f_2(i)=g_2(j)]$. Fix an injective pairing
$\mathbb{N}^{2}\to\mathbb{N}$ and set $F(i) = \langle f_1(i), f_2(i)\rangle$,
$G(j) = \langle g_1(j), g_2(j)\rangle$. Injectivity gives $F(i)=G(j)$ iff both coordinates agree,
so $(A\odot B)_{ij} = \bone[F(i)=G(j)]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Signed sums]
$A \in \Bcl_L$ means $A_{ij} = \sum_{\ell=1}^{L}\varepsilon_{\ell}(B_{\ell})_{ij}$ for all $i,j$,
with each $B_{\ell}$ a blow-up and $\varepsilon_{\ell}\in\{\pm 1\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Closure properties]\label{thm:closure}
Let $A \in \Bcl_{L_1}$ and $B \in \Bcl_{L_2}$ be of the same shape. Then $-A \in \Bcl_{L_1}$,
$A+B \in \Bcl_{L_1+L_2}$, $A \odot B \in \Bcl_{L_1L_2}$, $\bone - A \in \Bcl_{L_1+1}$, and
$\Bcl_L \subseteq \Bcl_{L'}$ for $L' \ge L$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Flip all signs for the first. Concatenate the two lists for the second. For the third expand
$A_{ij}B_{ij} = \sum_{\ell_1,\ell_2}(\varepsilon_{\ell_1}\varepsilon'_{\ell_2})
(B_{\ell_1}\odot B'_{\ell_2})_{ij}$ and use Theorem~\ref{thm:blowupclosed}. For the fourth, the
all-ones matrix is a blow-up (all labels equal), so $\bone - A = \bone + (-A)$. For the last, the
zero matrix is a blow-up (row $i$ gets label $i$, column $j$ gets label $n+m+j$, so labels never
match), and padding with $+0$ raises $L$ by one.
\end{proof}

Thus $\bigcup_L \Bcl_L$ is a subring for the Hadamard product, closed under complementation, and
the least number of terms behaves like a degree.

\section{The blow-up number and equality queries}\label{sec:eq}

\begin{definition}[Blow-up number]
$\eqc(A) := \min\{L : A \in \Bcl_L\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Universal bounds]\label{prop:universal}
Let $A$ be boolean $m\times n$. Then $A \in \Bcl_m$, $\eqc(A) \le \min(m,n)$ and
$\gtwo{A} \le \sqrt{\min(m,n)}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
For each row index $\ell$ let $B_{\ell}$ agree with $A$ on row $\ell$ and vanish elsewhere; each
$B_{\ell}$ is a blow-up (give row $\ell$ and every column $j$ with $A_{\ell j}=1$ a common tag and
all other rows and columns pairwise distinct fresh tags), and $A = \sum_{\ell} B_{\ell}$ with all
signs $+1$; transpose for $n$. For the norm, take $x_i = e_i \in \mathbb{R}^{m}$ and $y_j$ the
$j$-th column of $A$, so that the unbalanced product of maxima is $1\cdot\sqrt{m}$, and apply
Proposition~\ref{prop:balance}; transpose for $\sqrt n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Easy direction]\label{prop:easy}
For boolean $A$, $\gtwo{A} \le \eqc(A)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:universal} the minimum is attained, say $A = \sum_{\ell\le L}
\varepsilon_{\ell}B_{\ell}$ with $L = \eqc(A)$. Each term has $\gamma_2$-norm at most $1$ by
Proposition~\ref{prop:blowupcontr} and negation-invariance; apply subadditivity.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The unit level]\label{prop:unit}
For boolean $A$: $\eqc(A) \le 1$ if and only if $A$ is a blow-up.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
One direction is trivial. Conversely let $A = \varepsilon B$ with $B$ a blow-up. If
$\varepsilon = 1$ we are done; if $\varepsilon = -1$ then $A$ and $B$ boolean with $A = -B$ forces
$A = B = 0$, and the zero matrix is a blow-up.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Equality-query form]\label{thm:queries}
$A \in \Bcl_L$ if and only if there are labellings $f_{\ell} : [m]\to\mathbb{N}$,
$g_{\ell} : [n]\to\mathbb{N}$ and signs $\varepsilon_{\ell} \in \{\pm1\}$, $\ell = 1,\dots,L$,
with
\[
  A_{ij} = \sum_{\ell=1}^{L}\varepsilon_{\ell}\,\bone[\,f_{\ell}(i) = g_{\ell}(j)\,] .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Unwind the definition of a blow-up inside that of a signed sum: a family of blow-ups is exactly a
family of pairs of labellings, and conversely each equality indicator is a blow-up.
\end{proof}

Statement (2) is a communication protocol: Alice, holding $i$, and Bob, holding $j$, compute $L$
labels each, and $A_{ij}$ is a signed tally of $L$ equality tests. A bound $\eqc(A)\le L(\gamma)$
uniform in the dimensions places every family of bounded factorization norm in
$\mathrm{P}^{\mathrm{EQ}}$.

\begin{theorem}[The conjecture below the gap, with $L=1$]\label{thm:belowgap}
Let $\gamma < 2\sqrt3/3$. Then for all $m,n$ and every boolean $m\times n$ matrix $A$,
$\gtwo{A}\le\gamma$ implies $\eqc(A)\le 1$; that is, $A$ is a single blow-up of a partial identity
matrix.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:gap} makes $A$ a blow-up; Proposition~\ref{prop:unit} concludes.
\end{proof}

The content is the uniformity: the bound $L=1$ holds for all sizes simultaneously, with no
$\log^{*}n$ correction, and with an optimal threshold, since $\gtwo{T_2} = 2\sqrt3/3$ and
$\eqc(T_2) = 2$.

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Deciding the contractive case}
Theorem~\ref{thm:char} makes ``$\gtwo{A}\le 1$?'' decidable combinatorially. Group the rows by
their bit-patterns; rigidity holds iff, for every column $j$, all rows carrying a $1$ in column $j$
lie in one class. On success, output $f(i) = \min\{j : A_{ij}=1\}$ (fresh tag for a zero row) and
$g(j) = f(i_0)$ for any $i_0$ hitting $j$ (fresh tag for a zero column); these labels certify
$\gtwo{A}\le1$. On failure, output the witnessing $2\times2$ pattern, which by
Theorem~\ref{thm:sos} certifies $\gtwo{A}\ge 2\sqrt3/3$. Expected time $O(mn)$ with hashing. The
algorithm is self-certifying in both directions, and the absence of a third outcome is exactly the
gap theorem.

\subsection{Row decomposition}
Decompose $A$ as the sum of its rows, each of which is a blow-up; cost $O(mn)$, establishing
$\eqc(A)\le m$.

\subsection{Numerical evaluation of the norm}
$\gtwo{A}$ is a semidefinite program: minimize $c$ subject to
\[
  G = \begin{pmatrix} P & A \\ A^{\mathsf T} & Q\end{pmatrix} \succeq 0,
  \qquad P_{ii} \le c, \quad Q_{jj} \le c ,
\]
where $P$ and $Q$ are the Gram matrices of the $x_i$ and the $y_j$. Any feasible $G$ yields a
factorization by Cholesky. For small patterns, projected alternating minimization on the vectors,
or a direct parametrization of the few free Gram entries, suffices; both confirm the exact value
$2\sqrt3/3$ for $T_2$.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

\subsection{What the gap says}
The set $\Gamma = \{\gtwo{A} : A \text{ boolean, any size}\}$ contains $0$ and $1$, omits the whole
interval $(1, 2\sqrt3/3)$, and contains $2\sqrt3/3$. Near its bottom, $\Gamma$ is discrete, and the
discreteness is caused by a forbidden pattern: crossing the threshold requires embedding a
$2$-staircase, and the $2$-staircase costs exactly $2\sqrt3/3$. This is a template: any lower bound
``pattern $P$ forces $\gtwo{A}\ge\kappa_P$'' combines with a structure theorem ``no copy of $P$
implies a normal form'' to produce a gap.

\subsection{Relation to the general conjecture}
Proposition~\ref{prop:easy} gives $\gtwo{A}\le\eqc(A)$ in complete generality, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:belowgap} gives the optimal converse in the range $\gamma<2\sqrt3/3$, where
$L(\gamma)=1$. Hence
\[
  \eqc(A)\le 1 \iff \gtwo{A}\le 1,
  \qquad \eqc(A)\ge 2 \implies \gtwo{A} \ge \tfrac{2\sqrt3}{3} :
\]
needing a second equality query costs a definite amount of norm.

\subsection{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[A second gap]
The set of factorization norms of boolean matrices is discrete near its bottom: after $1$ and
$2\sqrt3/3$ the next attained value is $\gtwo{T_3}$, and no boolean matrix has norm strictly
between $2\sqrt3/3$ and $\gtwo{T_3}$.
\end{conjecture}

Crude numerical searches place $\gtwo{T_3}$ in the range $1.40$--$1.48$; two searches disagreed, so
no numerical value is claimed --- pinning it down exactly is part of the conjecture. The gap proof
reduces to pattern containment: a boolean matrix of norm $<2\sqrt3/3$ contains no $2$-staircase,
and the same reduction should turn ``norm $< \gtwo{T_3}$'' into ``contains no $3$-staircase'',
after which the finitely many staircase-free structures can be classified exactly as row rigidity
classifies the $2$-staircase-free case. The two-sided scheme used here --- an explicit
$k$-dimensional vector configuration against an explicit sum-of-squares certificate from the dual
semidefinite program --- transfers verbatim to $T_3$, whose program has only six free Gram
parameters.

\begin{conjecture}[Staircase growth is the only obstruction]
For boolean $A$, $\gtwo{A}\le\gamma$ implies that $A$ contains no staircase of length
$k > 2^{O(\gamma^{2})}$; conversely, a boolean matrix with no staircase of length $k$ satisfies
$\gtwo{A}\le f(k)$ for an explicit $f$.
\end{conjecture}

Row rigidity --- the condition characterizing $\gtwo{A}\le1$ --- is exactly ``no staircase of
length $2$'', so the whole conjecture on idempotent Schur multipliers may be a statement about a
forbidden-pattern hierarchy rather than about norms. The case $k=2$ is settled here in both
directions.

Further questions. The row decomposition gives $\eqc(T_k)\le k$, but this is already far from
optimal at $k=3$: with $f_1=(0,1,0)$, $g_1=(0,1,2)$, $f_2=(0,1,2)$, $g_2=(1,0,0)$ one has
\[
  (T_3)_{ij} = \bone[f_1(i)=g_1(j)] + \bone[f_2(i)=g_2(j)],
\]
so $\eqc(T_3)=2$ (and $\eqc(T_3)\ge2$ since $T_3$ is not a blow-up). What is the growth of
$\max\{\eqc(A) : \gtwo{A}\le\gamma\}$? Any family forcing $L(\gamma)\to\infty$ would be the first
genuine lower bound in Conjecture~\ref{conj:A}. Further: how does the factorization norm of a
complement compare with that of the matrix; is the submultiplicative bound
$\eqc(A\odot B)\le\eqc(A)\eqc(B)$ ever tight; and what is the trade-off when integer coefficients
of size up to $M$ replace signs?

